At each endpoint $t_j = j \/ 6$,

$ lr(|lambda \( t_j \)|) lt.eq b_j^(*) \, #h(2em) lr(|z \( t_j \)|) lt.eq a_j^(*) \, #h(2em) "Im" z \( t_j \) gt.eq g_j . $

On the interval $t_(j - 1) lt.eq t lt.eq t_j$, the corresponding bounds are

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.3)")
 $ lr(|lambda \( t \)|) lt.eq b_(j - 1)^(*) \, quad lr(|z \( t \)|) lt.eq a_(j - 1)^(*) \, quad "Im" z \( t \) gt.eq g_j + beta_j \( t_j - t \) \, quad lr(|u_n \( t \)|) \, lr(|v_n \( t \)|) lt.eq M_j . $ <interpolation:piece-envelopes>]

The density bounds hold uniformly for $n in { 0 } union cal(N)$.

Here are direct ways to verify these estimates. The identities

$ lr(|lambda \( t \)|) = frac(1, b sqrt(t^2 + h^2)) \, #h(2em) lr(|z \( t \)|)^2 = h^2 + frac(B^2 - 2 B h^2, t^2 + h^2) \, #h(2em) "Im" z \( t \) = h (frac(B, t^2 + h^2) - 1) $

show that the first two quantities decrease on $\[ 0 \, 1 \]$. The nonnegative quantity $2 h B t \/ \( t^2 + h^2 \)^2$, which is minus the derivative of $"Im" z$, has its minimum on each of the six intervals at an endpoint. Its only interior critical point on the positive axis is a maximum. These observations give the first three bounds in (3.3) by endpoint substitution. For the densities, $e^(i pi t_l n)$ depends only on $n$ modulo $12$, whereas $lr(|e^(- i pi t n)|) = 1$ on the real interval. The norm of an affine function of $t$ is convex. Thus an upper bound for both folded densities on the $j$th interval is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.4)")
 $ 2 max_(a in A) max {pi lr(|sum_(l = j)^6 P_l e^(i pi t_l a)|) \, quad pi^2 max_(u in { t_(j - 1) \, t_j }) lr(|sum_(l = j)^6 \( t_l - u \) P_l e^(i pi t_l a)|)} . $ <interpolation:density-check>]

Substitution gives a value less than the displayed $M_j$ in every case. For the constant input $C = 1$, the absolute folded atom masses are

$ \( m_0^(*) \, dots.h \, m_6^(*) \) = \( 5 \, 4 \, 4 \, 4 \, 2 \, 4 \, 2 \) . $

For $s gt.eq 0$, set

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.5)")
 $ L_j \( s \) & = b_(j - 1)^(*) e^(- pi g_j s) min {1 / 6 \, frac(1, pi beta_j s)} &  & \( 1 lt.eq j lt.eq 6 \) \,\
L_j^P \( s \) & = m_j^(*) b_j^(*) e^(- pi g_j s) &  & \( 0 lt.eq j lt.eq 6 \) . $ <interpolation:L>]

When $beta_j s = 0$, the minimum in the first line is understood to be $1 \/ 6$. Integrating the exponential envelope in (3.3) over a piece of length $1 \/ 6$ gives its factor $L_j \( s \)$. Differentiating the kernel contributes $\( i pi z \( t \) \)^ell$. Consequently, for every integer $ell gt.eq 0$ and every input $p$ of the form (2.11),

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.6)")
 $ lr(|K_p^(\( ell \)) \( s \)|) lt.eq w sum_(j = 1)^6 M_j L_j \( s \) \( pi a_(j - 1)^(*) \)^ell + lr(||) C sum_(j = 0)^6 L_j^P \( s \) \( pi a_j^(*) \)^ell \, #h(2em) s gt.eq 0 \, $ <interpolation:moment-bound>]

where $w = sum_(n in { 0 } union cal(N)) \( lr(|c_n|) + lr(|d_n|) \)$. The argument applies first to finite lists and then to all such lists by the finite-measure representation and absolute summability.

Both $Q_n$ and $D_n$ are $12$-periodic on the nodes. Moreover,

$ L_j \( n + 12 k \) lt.eq e^(- 12 k pi g_j) L_j \( n \) #h(2em) \( k gt.eq 0 \) \, $

because the minimum factor in (3.5) is nonincreasing. For every real cutoff $x gt.eq 0$, the value and derivative terms in (3.1), together with (3.6), therefore imply

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.7)")
 $ ∥S_(> x \, *)∥ lt.eq sum_(n in cal(N) ∩ \( x \, x + 12 \]) sum_(j = 1)^6 frac(1 + lr(|D_n|) + pi a_(j - 1)^(*), Q_n) thin frac(M_j L_j \( n \), 1 - e^(- 12 pi g_j)) . $ <interpolation:row-envelope>]

Here $> x$ means a strict inequality for the row node. This is a bound uniform over all input columns. It applies as well to a single input coefficient $c_0$ or $d_0$.

For completeness, this estimate also justifies the operator definition on the whole space. Apply the bound first to one positive-node basis column, then sum with the absolute coefficients of a finitely supported list. The case $x = 0$ bounds the resulting output norm by a constant times the input norm. Finite lists are dense in $X$, so this map has a unique bounded extension to $X$. The basis measures converge in total variation by Lemma 2; hence $K_(p_x) \( n \)$ and $K'_(p_x) \( n \)$ converge at each fixed node as the lists are truncated. The extended map therefore has exactly the coordinates in (3.1), not merely the coordinates of an auxiliary matrix limit.

For $x = 0 \, 4 \, 16 \, 100$, respectively, the right side of (3.7) is less than

$ 27.7 \, #h(2em) .272 \, #h(2em) .0145 \, #h(2em) 4.7 dot.op 10^(- 10) . $

We shall use the slightly weaker bounds

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.8)")
 $ ∥S_(> 0 \, *)∥ < 28 \, #h(2em) ∥S_(> 4 \, *)∥ < .28 \, #h(2em) ∥S_(> 16 \, *)∥ < .015 \, #h(2em) ∥S_(> 100 \, *)∥ < 5 dot.op 10^(- 10) . $ <interpolation:tails>]

For the input $C = 1$, the analogue of (3.7) replaces $M_j L_j$ by $L_j^P$, uses $a_j^(*)$ in place of $a_(j - 1)^(*)$, and sums over $0 lt.eq j lt.eq 6$. For rows greater than $100$, it gives the bound

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.9)")
 $ sum_(n in cal(N) \, med n > 100) (lr(|K_P \( n \)|) / Q_n + lr(|D_n K_P \( n \) - K'_P \( n \)|) / Q_n) < 3.2 dot.op 10^(- 8) . $ <interpolation:atom-tail>]

The scalar substitutions in these envelope estimates use only the explicit constants and the node data. Section 6.6 records the finite scalar bounds and the precise coverage of their supplied verifier.

#prodef([Remark 3.1.])[
  (Compactness of the interpolation operator).  Let $P_M$ keep the output coordinates with node at most $M$. Then $P_M S$ has finite rank. The right side of (3.7) tends to zero as $x arrow.r oo$: there are only six nodes in any period, the periodic $Q_n \, D_n$ are uniformly bounded with $Q_n$ bounded away from zero, and every $L_j \( n \)$ contains the factor $e^(- pi g_j n)$ with $g_j > 0$. Thus $∥S - P_M S∥ arrow.r 0$. The operator $S$ is a norm limit of finite-rank operators and is compact on $X$. This property describes the tail structure; the explicit inverse below is obtained independently from the quantitative block estimates.
] <interpolation:compactness>

=== 3.2 The finite certificates <the-finite-certificates>

There are $51$ nodes in $I_f$ and hence $102$ scalar coordinates. The approximate lists $tilde(x)_1 \, tilde(x)_2$ are given by Table 2, with the indicated factor $10^(- 10)$, and are zero at positive nodes beyond $100$. Append the fixed data (2.22) to obtain $tilde(p)_i$, and write $tilde(K)_i = K_(tilde(p)_i)$.

#thm([Lemma 3.2.])[
  (Finite certificates).  #emph[For each $eta in { 1 \, - 1 }$, the matrix $I - eta D$ is invertible, and 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.10)")
 $ ∥\( I - eta D \)^(- 1)∥ < 32 \, #h(2em) ∥\( I - eta D \)^(- 1) S_(J \, E)∥ < 3.7 \, #h(2em) ∥S_(E ∩ \[ 7 \, 16 \] \, J)∥ < .09 . $ <interpolation:finite-matrices>]

 For $i = 1 \, 2$ and $n in I_f$, the table satisfies 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.11)")
 $ lr(|tilde(c)_(i \, n) + frac(tilde(K)_(3 - i) \( n \), Q_n)|) & < 10^(- 9) \,\
lr(|tilde(d)_(i \, n) + upright(bold(1))_({ i = 1 \, med n = 1 }) + frac(tilde(K)'_(3 - i) \( n \) - D_n tilde(K)_(3 - i) \( n \), Q_n)|) & < 10^(- 9) . $ <interpolation:finite-residuals>]

 For each $i$, the norm of the tabulated list, including node zero, is less than $2.29$, and its norm on nodes greater than $40$ is less than $.00002$.]
] <interpolation:finite>

#proof[
  The primary interval calculation in Appendix 6 proves the exact bounds (6.13), (6.14), and (6.15), using the quadrature assertion of Lemma 16 for the analytic moment remainder. The elementary argument in Section 6.4 gives the matrix conclusions, and (6.2) gives the list norms. ◻
]

=== 3.3 Inversion on the whole coefficient space <inversion-on-the-whole-coefficient-space>

We first prove an inverse bound that keeps finite and tail residuals separate.

#thm([Lemma 3.3.])[
  #emph[For each $eta in { 1 \, - 1 }$, $I - eta S$ has a bounded inverse on $X$. If $r = r_f + r_t$, with $r_f$ supported on $I_f$ and $r_t$ on $cal(N) ∩ \( 100 \, oo \)$, then 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.12)")
 $ ∥\( I - eta S \)^(- 1) r∥_1 lt.eq 91 ∥r_f∥_1 + 2540 ∥r_t∥_1 . $ <interpolation:inverse-bound>]

]
] <interpolation:inverse>

#proof[
  Fix $eta$ and first restrict to the finite block $I_f$. Put
  $ V = \( I - eta D \)^(- 1) \, #h(2em) L = S_(E \, J) \, #h(2em) U = S_(J \, E) . $
  The first block of tail estimates and the finite certificates give
  $ ∥∥ L lt.eq ∥S_(E ∩ \[ 7 \, 16 \] \, J)∥ + ∥S_(E ∩ \( 16 \, 100 \] \, J)∥ < .09 + .015 < .11 . $
  Eliminating the $J$ coordinates from $\( I - eta S_(I_f \, I_f) \) x_f = r_f$ leaves on $E$ the operator
  $ I - eta S_(E \, E) - L V U . $
  Its difference from the identity has norm less than
  $ ∥S_(E \, E)∥ + ∥∥ L ∥V U∥ < .28 + .11 dot.op 3.7 = .687 < 1 . $
  It is therefore invertible by a Neumann series. Solving this block and substituting into the $J$ equations yields an inverse $V_f$ for the whole finite compression, with
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.13)")
   $ ∥V_f∥ & lt.eq max {32 + frac(\( 1 + 3.7 \) dot.op .11 dot.op 32, 1 - .687) \, quad frac(1 + 3.7, 1 - .687)}\
 & < 90 . $ <interpolation:finite-inverse-bound>]
  Indeed, the two entries in the maximum bound the output norm arising from a unit input on $J$ and on $E$, respectively.
  Now write $X = X_f xor X_t$, where $X_t$ is the infinite tail space. The full equation $\( I - eta S \) x = r$ is equivalent, after elimination of $x_f$, to
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.14)")
   $ #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] I - eta S_(t \, t) - S_(t \, f) V_f S_(f \, t) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] x_t = r_t + eta S_(t \, f) V_f r_f . $ <interpolation:tail-schur>]
  Put $delta = 5 dot.op 10^(- 10)$. By (3.8),
  $ ∥S_(t \, t)∥ < delta \, #h(2em) ∥S_(t \, f)∥ < delta \, #h(2em) ∥S_(f \, t)∥ < 28 . $
  The operator subtracted from the identity in (3.14) thus has norm less than
  $ delta \( 1 + 90 dot.op 28 \) = 2521 delta < 1 . $
  Its Neumann series converges in the bounded operators on $X_t$. Together with the finite inverse this proves invertibility on the full space $X$, without any passage to a limit of finite-section solutions. Quantitatively,
  $ ∥x_t∥_1 lt.eq frac(∥r_t∥_1 + 90 delta ∥r_f∥_1, 1 - 2521 delta) \, #h(2em) ∥x_f∥_1 lt.eq 90 ∥r_f∥_1 + 2520 ∥x_t∥_1 . $
  Adding these inequalities gives coefficients less than $91$ and $2540$ for $∥r_f∥_1$ and $∥r_t∥_1$, respectively, which proves (3.12). ◻
]

#thm([Proposition 3.4.])[
  (Exact interpolation and coefficient enclosure).  #emph[There are unique real lists $x_1 \, x_2 in X$ satisfying (2.25) with the fixed data (2.22). For each $i = 1 \, 2$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.15)")
 $ sum_(n in cal(N)) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] lr(|c_(i \, n) - tilde(c)_(i \, n)|) + lr(|d_(i \, n) - tilde(d)_(i \, n)|) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] < .00003 . $ <interpolation:coefficient-error>]

 The norm of each exact list, including its node-zero coefficients, is less than $2.3$. The corresponding functions satisfy all the interpolation conditions (2.24).]
] <interpolation:solution>

The uniqueness here is within the specified coefficient space, with the fixed node-zero data and constants. Triangular-shell values and first derivatives do not determine an arbitrary radial Schwartz function and its Fourier transform uniquely #cite(label("Sardari2021")) Theorem 1.7.

#proof[
  Let $p_i^0$ denote (2.11) with the fixed values $\( C_i \, c_(i \, 0) \, d_(i \, 0) \)$ and all positive-node coefficients zero. Let $e in X$ have its $d_1$ coordinate equal to one and all other coordinates zero. Define $g_i in X$ by
  $ \( g_i \)_(c \, n) = - frac(K_(p_(3 - i)^0) \( n \), Q_n) \, #h(2em) \( g_i \)_(d \, n) = frac(D_n K_(p_(3 - i)^0) \( n \) - K'_(p_(3 - i)^0) \( n \), Q_n) - upright(bold(1))_({ i = 1 }) e_(d \, n) . $
  The estimates for the node-zero and constant columns show that these are summable lists. The equations to solve are precisely
  $ x_1 - S x_2 = g_1 \, #h(2em) x_2 - S x_1 = g_2 . $
  Their sum is an equation for $I - S$, and their difference is an equation for $I + S$. Lemma 8 gives unique real solutions and hence unique $x_1 \, x_2$.
  It remains to locate these solutions relative to the table. Set
  $ rho_i = tilde(x)_i - S tilde(x)_(3 - i) - g_i . $
  There are $102$ scalar coordinates in $I_f$, so (3.11) gives
  $ ∥\( rho_i \)_f∥_1 < 1.1 dot.op 10^(- 7) = : R_f . $
  On the tail, both the tabulated positive-node list and the vector $e$ vanish. The density-column estimate, now including node zero, and (3.9) imply
  $ ∥\( rho_i \)_t∥_1 < 2.29 dot.op 5 dot.op 10^(- 10) + .017 dot.op 3.2 dot.op 10^(- 8) = : R_t . $
  Here $2.29$ bounds the whole tabulated coefficient list, and $.017$ bounds both fixed constants in absolute value.
  The errors $x_i - tilde(x)_i$ are recovered by taking half the sum and half the difference of the solutions with residuals $- \( rho_1 + rho_2 \)$ and $- \( rho_1 - rho_2 \)$. Applying (3.12) to both gives, for each list,
  $ ∥x_i - tilde(x)_i∥_1 lt.eq 2 \( 91 R_f + 2540 R_t \) = .00002860012 < .00003 . $
  This proves (3.15). Since the fixed node-zero coefficients agree, the total exact coefficient norm is less than $2.29 + .00003 < 2.3$. Finally, (2.25) is equivalent to (2.24) by Lemma 5. ◻
]

== 4 Global signs <sec:signs>

We now prove the global signs for the exact functions supplied by Proposition 9. The strict bounds will also identify their zeros within the respective sign domains. Write

$ sigma_1 = - 1 \, #h(2em) sigma_2 = 1 \, #h(2em) delta = 3 dot.op 10^(- 5) . $

The difference between each exact coefficient list and its tabulated list has norm less than $delta$. Their fixed coefficients at zero and their constants $C_i$ agree. Tildes will always denote the functions constructed from the finite tabulated lists, so that $tilde(H)_i = tilde(p)_i + K_(tilde(p)_(3 - i))$. We use the spectral envelopes of Section 3 throughout.

=== 4.1 Taylor quotients on half-gaps <taylor-quotients-on-half-gaps>

Set $cal(N)_0 = { 0 } union cal(N)$. For each $m in cal(N)_0 ∩ \[ 0 \, 40 \]$, let $m^(+)$ be its successor in $cal(N)_0$ and include

$ y = frac(m^(+) - m, 2) . $

If $m > 0$, also include $y = \( m^(-) - m \) \/ 2$, where $m^(-)$ is its predecessor. Thus $y$ is signed, and the associated half-gap is

$ I_(m \, y) = { m + y v : 0 lt.eq v lt.eq 1 } . $

For function index $2$ we use every such interval, with order $nu = 0$ at $m = 0$ and $nu = 2$ otherwise. For function index $1$ we use only those intervals contained in $\[ 1 \, oo \)$, with $nu = 1$ at $m = 1$ and $nu = 2$ otherwise. In particular, the only odd order occurs on the rightward half-gap from $1$. There are $22$ centers from $0$ through $40$. They give $43$ half-gaps for index $2$ and, after removing the two intervals not contained in $\[ 1 \, oo \)$, $41$ for index $1$, for a total of $84$ certified intervals.

Fix one of the specified triples $\( i \, m \, y \)$. For $s = m + u$ in its interval, we will prove positivity of

$ frac(sigma_i H_i \( m + u \), u^nu) . $

Here $u^0 = 1$, and for $nu > 0$ the quotient at $u = 0$ is interpreted by continuity. The interpolation conditions make the derivatives of $H_i$ of orders below $nu$ vanish at $m$, so this extension exists. Positivity of the quotient gives the required sign: every order is even except for the rightward half-gap from $1$, where $u gt.eq 0$. Figure 1 shows the node-to-midpoint intervals on which these quotients are studied.

