) <fig:half-gaps>

The tabulated function $tilde(H)_i$ approximates $H_i$ but need not reproduce its exact zero and slope at a node. We therefore compare the exact quotient with the Taylor series of $tilde(H)_i$ after deleting the terms of orders below $nu$. Put $Delta_i = H_i - tilde(H)_i$. Since $tilde(H)_i$ is entire and the lower derivatives of $H_i$ vanish, this Taylor series gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.1)")
 $ & frac(sigma_i H_i \( m + u \), u^nu) - sigma_i sum_(r = 0)^oo frac(tilde(H)_i^(\( r + nu \)) \( m \), \( r + nu \) !) u^r\
 & #h(1cm)  = sigma_i / u^nu (Delta_i \( m + u \) - sum_(j = 0)^(nu - 1) frac(Delta_i^(\( j \)) \( m \), j !) u^j) . $ <signs:deleted-jets>]

For $nu = 0$, the sum on the second line is empty and the right-hand side is simply $sigma_i Delta_i \( m + u \)$. For $nu > 0$, Taylor’s integral remainder gives the exact identity

$ frac(Delta_i \( m + u \) - sum_(j = 0)^(nu - 1) Delta_i^(\( j \)) \( m \) u^j \/ j !, u^nu) = frac(1, \( nu - 1 \) !) integral_0^1 \( 1 - t \)^(nu - 1) Delta_i^(\( nu \)) \( m + t u \) thin #h(-0.16667em) upright(d) t . $

It holds for either sign of $u$ and extends continuously to $u = 0$. Since $m + t u$ stays in the same half-gap, the absolute value of the right-hand side of (4.1) is at most

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.2)")
 $ frac(1, nu !) sup_(s in I_(m \, y)) lr(|Delta_i^(\( nu \)) \( s \)|) . $ <signs:coefficient-error>]

The same bound applies at $u = 0$ by continuity and for either orientation of the half-gap. Deleting the lower Taylor terms is essential here: dividing the raw table error by $u^nu$ could be singular at the node.

After writing $u = y v$, the first $29$ terms of the tabulated series in (4.1) form a polynomial of degree at most $28$ in $v$. We bound it on $\[ 0 \, 1 \]$ using the standard range enclosure by Bernstein coefficients #cite(label("TitiGarloff2019")) Section 3, retaining its elementary proof below.

#thm([Lemma 4.1.])[
  (Finite Bernstein certificate).  #emph[For every specified triple $\( i \, m \, y \)$, put 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.3)")
 $ T_r = sigma_i y^r frac(tilde(H)_i^(\( r + nu \)) \( m \), \( r + nu \) !) \, #h(2em) 0 lt.eq r lt.eq 28 . $ <signs:power-coefficients>]

 Then, for $0 lt.eq k lt.eq 28$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.4)")
 $ B_k := sum_(r = 0)^k binom(k, r) / binom(28, r) T_r > cases(delim: "{", 0.74 \, & m = 0 \,, 0.18 \, & m = 1 \,, 0.02 \, & m = 3 \, 4 \,, 0.009 \, & 4 < m lt.eq 40 .) $ <signs:bernstein-bound>]

]
] <signs:bernstein>

#proof[
  The primary interval calculation in Appendix 6 encloses the exact derivatives of the tabulated functions in (4.3), using the quadrature assertion of Lemma 16 for the analytic moment remainder. Its exact grouped bounds (6.16) imply the stated thresholds. ◻
]

The relevance of this finite statement is the identity

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.5)")
 $ sum_(r = 0)^28 T_r v^r = sum_(k = 0)^28 B_k binom(28, k) v^k \( 1 - v \)^(28 - k) . $ <signs:bernstein-identity>]

Indeed, for $0 lt.eq r lt.eq k lt.eq d = 28$, the binomial identity

$ binom(k, r) / binom(d, r) binom(d, k) = binom(d - r, k - r) $

gives

$ sum_(k = r)^d binom(k, r) / binom(d, r) binom(d, k) v^k \( 1 - v \)^(d - k) = v^r sum_(l = 0)^(d - r) binom(d - r, l) v^l \( 1 - v \)^(d - r - l) = v^r . $

Multiply by $T_r$, sum over $r$, and interchange the two finite sums to obtain (4.5). For $0 lt.eq v lt.eq 1$, the basis functions on the right are nonnegative and sum to one. Hence the lower bounds in (4.4) hold on the whole interval for the polynomial on the left. To obtain a lower bound for the exact quotient, it remains to control the coefficient error in (4.2) and the terms beyond degree $28$ in the tabulated series.

=== 4.2 Bounds for the two errors <bounds-for-the-two-errors>

The density bounds and (3.6), together with the separate coefficient error bounds $delta$ for the two functions, give, for $s gt.eq s_(*) gt.eq 0$,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.6)")
 $ lr(|Delta_i^(\( nu \)) \( s \)|) lt.eq delta sum_(j = 1)^6 M_j (frac(\( pi t_j \)^nu, 6) + L_j \( s_(*) \) \( pi a_(j - 1)^(*) \)^nu) . $ <signs:perturbation-envelope>]

There are no atom contributions because the constants $C_i$ have not changed. Substitution of the envelopes proves the following uniform bounds for (4.2):

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.7)")
 $ frac(1, nu !) sup_(I_(m \, y)) lr(|Delta_i^(\( nu \))|) < cases(delim: "{", 0.004 \, & m = 0 \,, 0.011 \, & m = 1 \,, 0.002 \, & m gt.eq 3 .) $ <signs:perturbation-caps>]

Here one uses $s_(*) = 0$ for $m = 0$, $s_(*) = 1 \/ 2$ for $m = 1$ and both possible orders, and $s_(*) = 2$ for $m gt.eq 3$. For example the first bound is $delta sum_(j = 1)^6 \( M_j \/ 6 + M_j L_j \( 0 \) \) < 0.004$. All these are bounds on intervals, obtained from the derivative envelopes.

It remains to truncate the infinite series in (4.1). Put $Y = lr(|y|)$ and $q = 29 + nu$. The total variation of the direct spectral measure of either tabulated function is less than $80$, since

$ 2.29 sum_(j = 1)^6 M_j / 6 + 0.017 sum_(j = 0)^6 m_j^(*) < 80 . $

For the transformed part, use coefficient norm less than $2.3$ and $lr(|C_i|) lt.eq 0.017$. Expanding each spectral exponential at $m$ gives the following bound for the omitted quotient terms:

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.8)")
 $ cal(E) \( m \, Y \, nu \) :=  & frac(Y^(- nu), q !) frac(1, 1 - pi a_0^(*) Y \/ \( q + 1 \)) #scale(x: 300%, y: 300%)[\(] 80 \( pi Y \)^q\
 & #h(2em) + 2.3 sum_(j = 1)^6 M_j L_j \( m \) \( pi a_(j - 1)^(*) Y \)^q + 0.017 sum_(j = 0)^6 L_j^P \( m \) \( pi a_j^(*) Y \)^q #scale(x: 300%, y: 300%)[\)] . $ <signs:taylor-bound>]

Indeed the first omitted exponential term has degree $q$ and subsequent absolute-value ratios are at most $pi a_0^(*) Y \/ \( q + 1 \) < 1$. After division by $u^nu$, all powers still have degree at least $29$, so their maximum for $lr(|u|) lt.eq Y$ is bounded by evaluating these absolute-value majorants at $Y$. This proves (4.8) also when $y < 0$.

The five representative substitutions are

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.9)")
 $ \( m \, Y \, nu \) = \( 0 \, 1 / 2 \, 0 \) \, med \( 1 \, 1 \, 1 \) \, med \( 1 \, 1 \, 2 \) \, med \( 3 \, 1 \, 2 \) \, med \( 4 \, 3 / 2 \, 2 \) . $ <signs:taylor-representatives>]

In each case (4.8) is less than $0.001$. Increasing $m$ or decreasing $Y$ only decreases the bound. The first three cases handle centers $0$ and $1$, the fourth handles $m = 3$, and the last handles every center $m gt.eq 4$. Thus the truncation error is less than $0.001$ on every required half-gap.

Combining (4.5), (4.1), (4.7), and (4.8), we obtain

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.10)")
 $ frac(sigma_i H_i \( s \), \( s - m \)^nu) > cases(delim: "{", 0.735 \, & m = 0 \,, 0.168 \, & m = 1 \,, 0.017 \, & m = 3 \, 4 \,, 0.006 \, & 4 < m lt.eq 40 \,) #h(2em) s in I_(m \, y) \, $ <signs:finite-quotient>]

with endpoint values interpreted by continuity. Since the only odd order has $s - m gt.eq 0$, these inequalities imply the desired signs on all of these intervals. Their union is $\[ 0 \, 41.5 \]$ for index $2$ and $\[ 1 \, 41.5 \]$ for index $1$: adjacent half-gaps meet at their common midpoint, and the last interval runs from $40$ halfway to $43$.

=== 4.3 The unbounded region <the-unbounded-region>

For $s gt.eq s_0 := 41.5$, split the exact direct part as

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.11)")
 $ p_i \( s \) & = P \( s \) R_(i \, 0) \( s \) + p_(i \, 1) \( s \) \,\
R_(i \, 0) \( s \) & = C_i + sum_(n in cal(N)_0\
n lt.eq 40) (frac(c_(i \, n), \( s - n \)^2) + frac(d_(i \, n), s - n)) \,\
tilde(R)_(i \, 0) \( s \) & = C_i + sum_(n in cal(N)_0\
n lt.eq 40) (frac(tilde(c)_(i \, n), \( s - n \)^2) + frac(tilde(d)_(i \, n), s - n)) . $ <signs:tail-split>]

Here $p_(i \, 1)$ is the cardinal contribution from exact coefficients at nodes greater than $40$. The function $R_(i \, 0)$ uses the exact coefficients, whereas $tilde(R)_(i \, 0)$ uses the finite table; their constants and node-zero coefficients agree. All denominators in these two rational functions are at least $1.5$. Write the corresponding decomposition of the exact function as

$ E_i := p_(i \, 1) + K_(p_(3 - i)) \, #h(2em) H_i = P R_(i \, 0) + E_i . $

For each $s gt.eq s_0$, the final comparison will use a nearest node $m gt.eq 43$. At such a node, the exact interpolation conditions and the double zero of $P$ make $E_i$ have zero value and slope; we verify this at the comparison step below. The useful scale for both terms is therefore $\( s - m \)^2$. We will bound $sigma_i R_(i \, 0)$ below, bound $P \( s \)$ below by a multiple of $\( s - m \)^2$, and bound $\| E_i \( s \) \|$ above at the same scale through a uniform second-derivative estimate.

We first show

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.12)")
 $ sigma_i R_(i \, 0) \( s \) > 0.011 #h(2em) \( s gt.eq s_0 \, med i = 1 \, 2 \) . $ <signs:rational-tail>]

For the tabulated coefficients put $u_n^(*) = sigma_i tilde(c)_(i \, n)$, $v_n^(*) = sigma_i tilde(d)_(i \, n)$, and $\[ w \]_(-) := min { w \, 0 }$. Using

$ frac(1, s - n) = 1 / s + frac(n, s \( s - n \)) $

and discarding positive terms gives the following lower bound for $sigma_i tilde(R)_(i \, 0) \( s \)$, uniformly for $s gt.eq s_0$:

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.13)")
 $ sigma_i C_i + frac(\[ sum_(n lt.eq 40) v_n^(*) \]_(-), s_0) + sum_(n lt.eq 40) (frac(\[ u_n^(*) \]_(-), \( s_0 - n \)^2) + frac(\[ v_n^(*) \]_(-) n, s_0 \( s_0 - n \))) \, $ <signs:rational-lower-sum>]

where both sums include $n = 0$. Each negative reciprocal term is smallest at $s = s_0$. The exact rational sums in (4.13) exceed $0.012$ for index $1$ and $0.016$ for index $2$. To pass to the exact rational part, use $s - n gt.eq 1.5 > 1$ and the coefficient enclosure to obtain

$ lr(|R_(i \, 0) \( s \) - tilde(R)_(i \, 0) \( s \)|) & lt.eq sum_(n lt.eq 40) (frac(lr(|c_(i \, n) - tilde(c)_(i \, n)|), \( s - n \)^2) + frac(lr(|d_(i \, n) - tilde(d)_(i \, n)|), s - n))\
 & < frac(delta, s_0 - 40) = 0.00002 . $

The finite lower sums therefore imply the asserted exact bound (4.12).

The coefficient norm of the tabulated part with $n > 40$ is less than $0.00002$ for either function. Hence the exact coefficient norm for $p_(i \, 1)$ is less than $0.00005$. The derivative envelopes imply

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.14)")
 $ sup_(s gt.eq s_0) lr(|E''_i \( s \)|) lt.eq  & 0.00005 dot.op 77 + 2.3 sum_(j = 1)^6 M_j L_j \( s_0 \) \( pi a_(j - 1)^(*) \)^2\
 & + 0.017 sum_(j = 0)^6 L_j^P \( s_0 \) \( pi a_j^(*) \)^2 < 0.006 . $ <signs:tail-second-derivative>]

For the direct part we used $sum_(j = 1)^6 M_j \( pi t_j \)^2 \/ 6 < 77$; for the transformed part we used the all-node coefficient norm less than $2.3$ from Proposition 9.

It remains to give the quadratic lower bound for $P$.

#thm([Lemma 4.2.])[
  #emph[If $m in 12 bb(Z) + A$ is a nearest zero of $P$ to a real number $s$, then 
$ P \( s \) gt.eq 0.68 \( s - m \)^2 . $

