=== 6.2 Moments and the integration rule <cert:quadrature>

For a finite input $p = P R$ supported on nodes at most $100$, put

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.5)")
 $ U_(p \, l) \( m \, y \) = frac(y^l p^(\( l \)) \( m \), l !) \, #h(2em) X_(p \, l) \( m \, y \) = frac(y^l K_p^(\( l \)) \( m \), l !) . $ <cert:moments>]

The domain for the quadrature error estimate is stated explicitly in Lemma 16.

We use the first Fejér quadrature rule, whose cosine-sum weights are described in #cite(label("Waldvogel2006")) Section 2, formula (2.3). Our weights are half the mass-two weights there, normalized to average over an interval; for even $N = 256$ the terminal cosine term in that formula vanishes. We prove the exactness, positivity and error bound needed here. Set $N = 256$, and for $0 lt.eq v < N$ define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.6)")
 $ alpha_v & = frac(v + 1 \/ 2, N) \, #h(2em) o_v = 1 / N (1 - 2 sum_(a = 1)^127 frac(cos \( 2 a pi alpha_v \), 4 a^2 - 1)) \,\
tau_(j v) & = frac(2 j - 1 + cos \( pi alpha_v \), 12) #h(2em) \( 1 lt.eq j lt.eq 6 \) . $ <cert:rule>]

Integration over $\( t_(j - 1) \, t_j \)$ is replaced by evaluation at $tau_(j v)$ with weights $o_v \/ 6$. To check exactness, expand a polynomial in the Chebyshev basis $T_k \( cos theta \) = cos \( k theta \)$. Discrete cosine orthogonality at $theta_v = pi alpha_v$ recovers its coefficients through degree $255$. The integral averages of this basis on $\[ - 1 \, 1 \]$ are zero at odd $k$ and $1 \/ \( 1 - k^2 \)$ at even $k$. These are exactly the coefficients in (6.6), so the rule is exact through degree $255$. Moreover,

$ o_v gt.eq 1 / 256 (1 - sum_(a = 1)^127 frac(2, 4 a^2 - 1)) = frac(1, 256 dot.op 255) > 0 \, #h(2em) sum_v o_v = 1 . $

Thus the six pieces together have total weight one.

For clarity, the complete density on piece $j$ used in this appendix is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.7)")
 $ f_j \( t \) = 2 pi sum_n e^(- i pi t n) {- pi c_n sum_(l = j)^6 \( t_l - t \) P_l e^(i pi t_l n) + i d_n sum_(l = j)^6 P_l e^(i pi t_l n)} . $ <cert:density>]

It includes node $0$ when that coefficient is part of the input. The folded atoms have masses $e_j C P_j$ at $t_j$, where $e_0 = 1$ and $e_j = 2$ for $j > 0$. The factors multiplying this density and these masses for $U$ and $X$, respectively, are

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.8)")
 $ e^(i pi t m) frac(\( i pi t y \)^l, l !) \, #h(2em) lambda \( t \) e^(i pi z \( t \) m) frac(\( i pi z \( t \) y \)^l, l !) . $ <cert:analytic-factors>]

The real part is taken after summation.

#thm([Lemma 6.1.])[
  (Quadrature moment error).  #emph[Let $p = P R$ have a real coefficient list supported in ${ 0 } union I_f$, with $parallel \( c \, d \) parallel_1 lt.eq 3$, and let $C in bb(R)$ satisfy $\| C \| lt.eq 1$. Let $m \, y in bb(R)$ and $l in { 0 \, dots.h \, 30 }$ satisfy $0 lt.eq m lt.eq 100$ and $\| y \| lt.eq 3 \/ 2$. The quadrature rule (6.6), with exact evaluations at its nodes and at the atoms, has error less than $10^(- 22)$ in each moment $U_(p \, l) \( m \, y \)$ and $X_(p \, l) \( m \, y \)$.]
] <cert:moment-error>

This domain contains all basis inputs and the two tabulated inputs used below.

#proof[
  Put $rho = 1 \/ 6$, $c_j = \( 2 j - 1 \) \/ 12$, $a_j = c_j + i h$, and $Delta_j = \| a_j \|^2 - rho^2$. These last quantities increase from $Delta_1 = 167 \/ 1200$ to $Delta_6 = 389 \/ 400$, so the disk $\| t - c_j \| lt.eq rho$ does not contain the pole $- i h$. All factors in (6.7) and (6.8) are analytic there. The reciprocal-disk calculation is exact: for $zeta = 1 \/ \( t + i h \)$,
  $ \| 1 - a_j zeta \|^2 - rho^2 \| zeta \|^2 = Delta_j lr(|zeta - overline(a)_j / Delta_j|)^2 - rho^2 / Delta_j . $
  Thus inversion maps the disk for $t + i h$ onto the disk with center $\( c_j - i h \) \/ Delta_j$ and radius $rho \/ Delta_j$. In particular,
  $ \| zeta \| lt.eq frac(\| a_j \| + rho, Delta_j) = frac(1, \| a_j \| - rho) . $
  The right side decreases with $c_j$, and $\| a_1 \| = sqrt(601) \/ 60$. Consequently the following are analytic bounds on every disk:
  $ \| t \| & lt.eq 13 / 12 < 1.1 \,\
\| lambda \( t \) \| & lt.eq frac(120, sqrt(3) \( sqrt(601) - 10 \)) < 4.774 < 5 \,\
\| z \( t \) \| & lt.eq frac(80, sqrt(601) - 10) + 2 / 5 < 5.912 < 6.2 . $
  For the two finer comparisons, it suffices to square the rational lower bounds $1.732^2 = 2.999824 < 3$ and $24.515^2 = 600.985225 < 601$, and then use
  $ 4.774 \( 1.732 \) \( 24.515 - 10 \) = 120.01826452 > 120 \,\
\( 5.912 - .4 \) \( 24.515 - 10 \) = 80.00668 > 80 . $
  The image disk for $z = - B zeta - i h$ has center $- B \( c_j - i h \) \/ Delta_j - i h$ and radius $B rho \/ Delta_j$. Its lowest imaginary part is therefore $B \( h - rho \) \/ Delta_j - h$. Since $h > rho$, this decreases with $Delta_j$, giving
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.9)")
   $ "Im" z \( t \) gt.eq B frac(h - 1 \/ 6, c_j^2 + h^2 - \( 1 \/ 6 \)^2) - h gt.eq - 1402 / 17505 > - .081 . $ <cert:disk-imaginary>]
  Since $sum_(l = 1)^6 \| P_l \| = 10$ and $\| t_l - t \| lt.eq 13 \/ 12$ for $l gt.eq j$, the analytic continuation of (6.7) on this disk is bounded by
  $ 3 max { 2 pi^2 dot.op 10 dot.op 13 \/ 12 \, thin 2 pi dot.op 10 } e^(100 pi \/ 6) < 1000 e^(100 pi \/ 6) . $
  Use $\| a^l \/ l ! \| lt.eq e^(\| a \|)$ for the divided powers. Including all factors, the direct and transformed integrands are bounded, respectively, by
  $ 1000 e^(200 pi \/ 6 + 1.5 pi dot.op 1.1) & < 5.38 dot.op 10^50 \,\
5000 e^(100 pi \/ 6 + 100 pi dot.op .081 + 1.5 pi dot.op 6.2) & < 1.51 dot.op 10^50 . $
  We may therefore use the common bound $M = 10^53$. The real interval has half-length $1 \/ 12$, half the disk radius. Cauchy’s coefficient estimate bounds the tail of the Taylor polynomial of degree $255$ by $M sum_(k = 256)^oo 2^(- k) = M 2^(- 255)$. Exactness, positivity and total mass one for the integral and quadrature rule give discrepancy at most
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.10)")
   $ 2^(- 254) 10^53 < 3.455 dot.op 10^(- 24) < 10^(- 22) . $ <cert:quadrature-error>]
