=== 6.3 Interval evaluation <cert:evaluation>

For a finite input $p$ in the real finite-input domain of Lemma 16 and a node row $m in I_f$, form the two real numbers

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.11)")
 $ cal(S)_m \( p \) = (- frac(X_(p \, 0) \( m \, 1 \), Q_m) \, #h(0.27778em) frac(D_m X_(p \, 0) \( m \, 1 \) - X_(p \, 1) \( m \, 1 \), Q_m)) . $ <cert:pair>]

With input $c_n = 1$ or $d_n = 1$ and all other data zero, these are the entries of the corresponding column of $S$ at row node $m$.

Let $D = S_(J \, J)$ and $U = S_(J \, E)$.

For the Bernstein certificates, use exactly the triples $\( i \, m \, y \)$ and orders $nu$ specified in Lemma 10; in particular $y$ is signed and no leftward interval at $m = 1$ is used for index $1$. Express its power coefficients in terms of the moments as

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.12)")
 $ T_r = sigma_i y^(- nu) (U_(tilde(p)_i \, r + nu) \( m \, y \) + X_(tilde(p)_(3 - i) \, r + nu) \( m \, y \)) . $ <cert:power-from-moments>]

The signed scaling is exact. Definition (6.5) gives $U_(p \, l) \( m \, y \) = y^l U_(p \, l) \( m \, 1 \)$ and the same identity for $X$. Therefore, for positive or negative $y$,

$ T_r = sigma_i y^r (U_(tilde(p)_i \, r + nu) \( m \, 1 \) + X_(tilde(p)_(3 - i) \, r + nu) \( m \, 1 \)) . $

This is the identity used when the implementation caches unscaled moments at $y = 1$; it does not discard the orientation of a half-gap.

Equations (6.6), (6.7), (6.8), (6.11), and (6.12) already specify all sums in the finite certificates below. The following pseudocode records their algebraic organization. A measure is a list of pairs $\( t \, g \)$ in the order used by the supplied interval implementation: first the $6 dot.op 256$ quadrature pairs $\( tau_(j v) \, o_v f_j \( tau_(j v) \) \/ 6 \)$, with $j = 1 \, dots.h \, 6$ outermost and $v = 0 \, dots.h \, 255$ innermost, and then the seven atom pairs $\( t_j \, e_j C P_j \)$ for $j = 0 \, dots.h \, 6$. The atom masses are zero for a basis input. The pseudocode is not a byte-for-byte execution trace. The supplied source fixes the detailed arithmetic and summation order for an actual interval run, including the factoring and caching described below.

#raw(block: true, lang: "", "Moments(measure, m, y, L):\n    U[0..L] = 0; X[0..L] = 0\n    for (t,g) in measure:\n        lam = i/(b*(t+i*h))\n        z = -B/(t+i*h)-i*h\n        u = g*exp(i*pi*t*m)\n        v = g*lam*exp(i*pi*z*m)\n        for r = 0..L:\n            U[r] += real(u); X[r] += real(v)\n            u *= (i*pi*t*y)/(r+1)\n            v *= (i*pi*z*y)/(r+1)\n    return U,X\n\nPair(measure,m):\n    U,X = Moments(measure,m,1,1)\n    return -X[0]/Q[m], (D[m]*X[0]-X[1])/Q[m]\n\nfor basis column c[n] or d[n], n in I_f:\n    measure = Measure(basis column; all other data zero)\n    column = stack Pair(measure,m), m in J\n    if n in J:\n        also form rows m in E intersect [7,16]\n    test column sums of W[eta] and the defect/product matrices\n\nfor i in {1,2}, m in I_f:\n    c,d = Pair(Measure(full table input 3-i),m)\n    test abs(table_c[i,m]-c)\n    test abs(table_d[i,m]+indicator(i==1 and m==1)-d)\n\nfor (i,m,y,nu) in the prescribed half-gap cases:\n    U,X = Moments for the two full table inputs, through L=30\n    T[r] = sigma[i]*(U[i,r+nu]+X[3-i,r+nu])/y**nu\n    for k = 0..28:\n        Bk = T[0]; a = 1\n        for r = 1..k:\n            a *= (k+1-r)/(29-r)\n            Bk += a*T[r]\n        test the appropriate lower bound for Bk")

Here #raw("stack") means all first entries followed by all second entries. The recurrence for $a$ equals $binom(k, r) \/ binom(28, r)$. #raw("Measure") denotes this finite list and introduces no additional approximation. The fixed data at zero are used only for the two full table inputs. Reusing a measure means reusing its whole finite list.

For the accompanying certified evaluation, every real or complex quantity in these sums is enclosed using interval arithmetic. The pair calculation forms the moments at $y = 1$. For the Bernstein calculation, the implementation also forms and caches the unscaled moments at $y = 1$, then uses the signed $y^r$ identity following (6.12). Each unscaled real moment enclosure is enlarged by $\[ - 10^(- 22) \, 10^(- 22) \]$, as justified by Lemma 16. Consequently the analytic-remainder contribution to the enclosure radius of a Bernstein coefficient is bounded by

$ 2 dot.op 10^(- 22) sum_(r = 0)^k binom(k, r) / binom(28, r) \| y \|^r & lt.eq 2 dot.op 10^(- 22) sum_(r = 0)^28 \( 3 \/ 2 \)^r\
 & = 4 dot.op 10^(- 22) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( 3 \/ 2 \)^29 - 1 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] < 5.2 dot.op 10^(- 17) . $

The last inequality is an exact rational comparison. It is not correct to regard $10^(- 22)$ as an unamplified per-Bernstein error. Arb rounding and dependency effects can further widen the balls; all of them are propagated by the subsequent products, absolute values, column sums, and Bernstein combinations. An upper bound is accepted only when the complete enclosure lies strictly below the asserted rational bound, and a lower bound only when it lies strictly above. This procedure uses enclosures of the exact integrals, not a quadrature error inferred from observed numerical agreement.

The ball-arithmetic inclusion methodology is described in #cite(label("Johansson2017")). The supplied implementation uses Arb through python-flint at $192$ bits of precision; the citation does not by itself certify this local environment, its call domains, or a run. The entry point runs two verifiers in the same directory, and . Their output records, and , and the aggregate record are placed in its subdirectory. The first verifier checks the exact bounds in (6.13), (6.14), (6.15), and (6.16), as well as all 2436 individual Bernstein inequalities in Lemma 10. It also asserts four finite brackets: the two defect brackets, the exterior bracket, and the bracket for $\( 2 \, 16 \, - 1 \/ 2 \, 28 \)$. All its comparisons and extrema use complete enclosures.

The mathematical $W$ argument in the next subsection uses only products and column sums. The supplied program also calls a certified inverse for $I - eta D$ and tests its inverse and cross-product norms as additional checks; these calls are not needed for the $W$ deduction.

