For every signed half-gap in Lemma 10, $y eq.not 0$ and $\| y \|^(- nu) lt.eq 4$. Directly from (6.5),

$ y^(- nu) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] U_(p \, r + nu) \( m \, y \) + X_(q \, r + nu) \( m \, y \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = y^r #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] U_(p \, r + nu) \( m \, 1 \) + X_(q \, r + nu) \( m \, 1 \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $

This identity also holds for negative $y$ and gives exactly the power coefficients of (6.12); it does not replace their derivatives by interpolated jets. Two moment errors less than $10^(- 14)$ give a $T_r$ error less than $8 dot.op 10^(- 14)$. For fixed $0 lt.eq k lt.eq 28$, the rational recurrence $a_0 = 1$ and $a_r = a_(r - 1) \( k + 1 - r \) \/ \( 29 - r \)$ satisfies

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.7)")
 $ a_r = product_(j = 1)^r frac(k + 1 - j, 29 - j) = frac(k !, \( k - r \) !) frac(\( 28 - r \) !, 28 !) = binom(k, r) / binom(28, r) #h(2em) \( 0 lt.eq r lt.eq k \) . $ <ind:bernstein-multiplier>]

Each factor is in $\[ 0 \, 1 \]$. There are $sum_(k = 0)^28 k = 28 dot.op 29 \/ 2 = 406$ nonconstant multipliers, in addition to $a_0 = 1$ for each $k$. Every Bernstein sum has at most $29$ terms, so its error is strictly less than $29 \( 8 dot.op 10^(- 14) \) = 2.32 dot.op 10^(- 12)$.

