=== 7.6 Rational-center Taylor integration <ind:taylor>

For a finite input in the real moment domain of Lemma 16, let $F_(j \, ell)^U$ and $F_(j \, ell)^X$ be the density (6.7) multiplied by the respective factors in (6.8). The input is supported on nodes at most $100$, has coefficient-list norm at most $3$ and $\| C \| lt.eq 1$, and the parameters satisfy $0 lt.eq m lt.eq 100$, $\| y \| lt.eq 3 \/ 2$, and $ell in { 0 \, dots.h \, 30 }$. The coefficients, $C \, m \, y$ are rational when a rational enclosure is requested. The six centers and the two radii are

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.15)")
 $ c_j = frac(2 j - 1, 12) \, #h(2em) R = 1 / 6 \, #h(2em) a = 1 / 12 quad \( 1 lt.eq j lt.eq 6 \) . $ <ind:centers>]

Each $F_(j \, ell)^U$ and $F_(j \, ell)^X$ is analytic on a neighborhood of $\| t - c_j \| lt.eq R$: the input list is finite and the only rational-kernel pole, $- i h$, is outside that disk. This analytic assertion is made for finite inputs. An arbitrary $ell^1$ list need not give a complex-frequency density analytic on these disks.

#thm([Lemma 7.4.])[
  (Taylor averages with rational coefficient radii).  #emph[Suppose $F \( c_j + x \) = sum_(k gt.eq 0) A_k x^k$ is analytic on a neighborhood of $\| x \| lt.eq R$ and $\| F \| lt.eq M$ there. Let $D gt.eq 0$ be an integer. For $0 lt.eq k lt.eq D$, suppose $hat(A)_k in bb(Q) \( i \)$ and $e_k in bb(Q)_(gt.eq 0)$ satisfy $\| A_k - hat(A)_k \| lt.eq e_k$. Then 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.16)")
 $ lr(|6 integral_(t_(j - 1))^(t_j) F \( t \) thin #h(-0.16667em) upright(d) t - sum_(2 r lt.eq D) hat(A)_(2 r) frac(a^(2 r), 2 r + 1)|) lt.eq M 2^(- D) + sum_(2 r lt.eq D) e_(2 r) frac(a^(2 r), 2 r + 1) . $ <ind:taylor-average-error>]

 If the final sum of radii is at most $delta$ for each of the six pieces, their combined integral has error at most $M 2^(- D) + delta$. Discrete atoms are not included in this estimate and are to be enclosed separately.]
] <ind:taylor-average>

#proof[
  Since $2 a = 1 \/ 6$, the expression $6 integral F$ is the interval average. The average of $x^k$ on $\[ - a \, a \]$ is zero for odd $k$ and is $a^k \/ \( k + 1 \)$ for even $k$. Thus the degree-$D$ Taylor polynomial has the exact average
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.17)")
   $ sum_(2 r lt.eq D) A_(2 r) frac(a^(2 r), 2 r + 1) . $ <ind:even-average>]
  Cauchy’s estimate gives $\| A_k \| lt.eq M R^(- k)$. Bounding the average of the absolute tail by its supremum on $\[ - a \, a \]$ gives the conservative bound
  $ M sum_(k > D) \( a \/ R \)^k = M sum_(k > D) 2^(- k) = M 2^(- D) . $
  Replacing the even coefficients adds the displayed weighted sum of their radii. Finally, each of the six integrals is $1 \/ 6$ times its average, so the six factors $1 \/ 6$ sum to one. Taking a real part cannot increase the error. Atoms have no integral tail of this kind. ◻
]

Here are explicit rational operations for obtaining the coefficient enclosures in this lemma. Write $cal(B) \( a \, r \) = { w in bb(C) : \| w - a \| lt.eq r }$, where $a in bb(Q) \( i \)$ and $r in bb(Q)_(gt.eq 0)$, and put $chevron.l a chevron.r_1 = \| Re a \| + \| Im a \|$. The following enclosure operations use rational centers and radii:

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.18)")
 $ cal(B) \( a \, r \) + cal(B) \( b \, s \) & subset.eq cal(B) \( a + b \, r + s \) \,\
cal(B) \( a \, r \) cal(B) \( b \, s \) & subset.eq cal(B) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] a b \, chevron.l a chevron.r_1 s + chevron.l b chevron.r_1 r + r s #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \,\
cal(B) \( a \, r \)^(- 1) & subset.eq cal(B) \( a^(- 1) \, r \/ ell_0^2 \) quad upright("if ") 0 < ell_0 lt.eq \| a \| - r . $ <ind:ball-operations>]

In the last line $a^(- 1) in bb(Q) \( i \)$, and the condition on a rational $ell_0$ can be checked by squaring rational quantities. The bound follows from $\| w^(- 1) - a^(- 1) \| = \| w - a \| \/ \( \| w \| \| a \| \)$. These operations allow repeated use of the same enclosure; possible dependence between its occurrences does not invalidate inclusion.

Constant exponentials also admit arbitrarily small rational radii. Use the rational bounds $cal(T)_n \, cal(E)_n$ from (7.2). If $w in cal(B) \( w_0 \, r \)$, choose rational $q gt.eq \| w_0 \|$, for example $q = chevron.l w_0 chevron.r_1$, and nonnegative integers $n \, s$ with $q < n + 2$ and $q + r < s + 2$. Then

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.19)")
 $ e^w in cal(B) (sum_(k = 0)^n frac(w_0^k, k !) \, cal(T)_n \( q \) + r cal(E)_s \( q + r \)) . $ <ind:constant-exponential>]

Indeed, the first radius bounds the complex Taylor tail at $w_0$. The integral first-difference formula for $e^w$ bounds the change from $w_0$ to $w$ by $r e^(q + r)$, giving the second radius. Both preconditions in (7.2) are explicit; no estimate of a positive exponential by a truncated sum alone is being used. For bounded $q$, $cal(T)_n \( q \) arrow.r 0$ as $n arrow.r oo$.

For clarity, the coefficient recurrences needed for the present integrands are as follows. If $a \( x \) = sum a_k x^k$ and $b \( x \) = sum b_k x^k$, multiplication is convolution,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.20)")
 $ \( a b \)_k = sum_(v = 0)^k a_v b_(k - v) . $ <ind:convolution>]

If $a_v in cal(B) \( hat(a)_v \, e_v \)$ and $b_v in cal(B) \( hat(b)_v \, f_v \)$, a rational radius for the center $sum_(v = 0)^k hat(a)_v hat(b)_(k - v)$ is explicitly

$ sum_(v = 0)^k (chevron.l hat(a)_v chevron.r_1 f_(k - v) + chevron.l hat(b)_(k - v) chevron.r_1 e_v + e_v f_(k - v)) . $

For a reciprocal series $q \( x \) = a \( x \)^(- 1)$ with $a_0 eq.not 0$, and for an exponential series $E \( x \) = e^(g \( x \))$, the recurrences are

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.21)")
 $ q_0 & = a_0^(- 1) \, & q_k & = - q_0 sum_(v = 1)^k a_v q_(k - v) quad \( k gt.eq 1 \) \,\
E_0 & = e^(g_0) \, & E_k & = 1 / k sum_(v = 1)^k v g_v E_(k - v) quad \( k gt.eq 1 \) . $ <ind:reciprocal-exponential-recurrences>]

The second line follows by comparing coefficients in $E' = g' E$. Use (7.19) for $E_0$ and (7.18) in these recurrences. Along with the displayed convolution radius, this specifies a rational radius for every coefficient, not merely a formal power series.

More particularly, $d_j = c_j + i h in bb(Q) \( i \)$ and

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.22)")
 $ r_0 = d_j^(- 1) \, quad r_(k + 1) = - r_k \/ d_j \, #h(2em) lambda_k = i r_k \/ b \, quad z_0 = - B r_0 - i h \, quad z_k = - B r_k med \( k gt.eq 1 \) $ <ind:kernel-recurrences>]

are the coefficients of $\( t + i h \)^(- 1)$, $lambda \( t \)$, and $z \( t \)$ at $t = c_j + x$. The $r_k \, z_k$ are rational complex numbers; an enclosure for $b^(- 1)$ gives the radii for $lambda_k$. On piece $j$, put

$ L_(n \, j) = sum_(v = j)^6 P_v e^(i pi t_v n) \, #h(2em) H_(n \, j) = sum_(v = j)^6 t_v P_v e^(i pi t_v n) . $

For $Phi_n \( x \) = e^(- i pi n \( c_j + x \))$, use $phi.alt_(n \, 0) = e^(- i pi n c_j)$ and $phi.alt_(n \, k + 1) = \( - i pi n \) phi.alt_(n \, k) \/ \( k + 1 \)$. The density is then the finite series expression

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.23)")
 $ f_j \( c_j + x \) = 2 pi sum_n Phi_n \( x \) {- pi c_n #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] H_(n \, j) - \( c_j + x \) L_(n \, j) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] + i d_n L_(n \, j)} . $ <ind:density-recurrence>]

The constant exponentials in $L_(n \, j) \, H_(n \, j) \, phi.alt_(n \, 0)$ are enclosed by (7.19). For a series $v \( x \)$ equal to $c_j + x$ or $z \( c_j + x \)$, the divided powers obey

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.24)")
 $ V_0 \( x \) = 1 \, #h(2em) V_(ell + 1) \( x \) = frac(V_ell \( x \) thin i pi y v \( x \), ell + 1) . $ <ind:divided-power-recurrence>]

Finally apply the exponential recurrence to $g = i pi m \( c_j + x \)$ or $g = i pi m z \( c_j + x \)$, and multiply the resulting series by the density, the divided power, and, in the transformed case, $lambda$. Truncating these recurrences after degree $D$ gives every coefficient through $D$ exactly at the level of formal series; omitted higher coefficients cannot enter a lower convolution coefficient.

The seven possible atoms are handled separately. At $t_j$, enclose their masses $e_j C P_j$ and the appropriate factor in (6.8) by the same rational operations and constant exponential bound. If their respective centers and radii are $\( a_j \, r_j \)$ and $\( g_j \, s_j \)$, their product has radius

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.25)")
 $ eta_j = chevron.l a_j chevron.r_1 s_j + chevron.l g_j chevron.r_1 r_j + r_j s_j . $ <ind:atom-radii>]

The sum $eta = sum_(j = 0)^6 eta_j$ is added to the six-piece error; there is no Taylor integration tail for an atom. When $C = 0$, all these terms vanish.

All of these radii can be refined effectively. Alternating rational arctangent sums in Machin’s identity enclose $pi$ to arbitrarily small width, and rational bisection of the positive square root of $3 \/ 4$ does the same for $b$. The kernel denominators have $\| c_j + i h \| gt.eq h = 2 \/ 5$, and $b > 4 \/ 5$. Taking real and imaginary parts of the constant exponential enclosures also encloses cosine and sine with the same radius. The nonzero sine denominators in (2.8) have modulus greater than $1 \/ 4$. Consequently sufficiently refined enclosures certify fixed positive lower bounds for the moduli of all denominators used above or in computing $Q_m \, D_m$, and separately a positive lower bound for $Q_m$ at pair division. For each fixed $D$ there are finitely many operations in (7.20)–(7.24). Their radius formulas tend to zero when their input radii tend to zero, while (7.19) has radius tending to zero by refining its input and increasing its two truncation orders. Thus one may refine the rational inputs and exponential orders, recompute the displayed rational radii, and stop when any prescribed finite collection of positive coefficient and atom budgets holds. The convergence just proved ensures that this rational stopping test terminates. This is a computability statement for each specified finite set of inputs, not a claim that any of its moment sums have been evaluated.

We next make sufficient budgets explicit. Put $h_D = floor.l D \/ 2 floor.r + 1$. For rational $delta > 0$, the conditions

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.26)")
 $ e_(j \, 2 r) lt.eq frac(delta \( 2 r + 1 \) 12^(2 r), h_D) quad \( 0 lt.eq 2 r lt.eq D \) $ <ind:coefficient-budget>]

on each piece and moment imply that the weighted coefficient-radius sum in (7.16) is at most $delta$.

We now combine these integration budgets with the pair estimate (7.6) and the Bernstein estimate from Section 7.3.1.

#thm([Proposition 7.5.])[
  (Two conditional Taylor budgets).  #emph[The following choices give sufficient rational error enclosures for the same finite quantities used in Appendix 6.]
  #emph[For a basis input at $n in J = { 1 \, 3 \, 4 }$, a row $m in J$, $y = 1$, and $ell = 0 \, 1$, take $D = 63$, $h_D = 32$, $M = 10^6$, and $delta = 10^(- 14)$ in (7.26). There are no atoms. If $Q_m \, D_m$ have rational errors at most $10^(- 25)$, either entry of (6.11) has error less than $2.13 dot.op 10^(- 13) < 10^(- 12)$.]
  #emph[For the full rational finite-input domain at the start of this subsection, take $D = 255$, $h_D = 128$, $M = 10^53$, and $delta = 4 dot.op 10^(- 15)$ in (7.26), and require each of the seven atom-product radii in (7.25) to be at most $4 dot.op 10^(- 15) \/ 7$. Each $U$ and $X$ moment then has error less than $10^(- 14)$. For node rows $m in I_f$, with rational $Q_m \, D_m$ errors at most $10^(- 25)$, either entry of (6.11) has error less than $1.8 dot.op 10^(- 14)$.]
] <ind:taylor-budgets>

#proof[
  The first-block majorant $10^6$ is proved in Section 7.5.1. Lemma 20 gives
  $ epsilon.alt lt.eq 10^6 2^(- 63) + 10^(- 14) < 1.19 dot.op 10^(- 13) . $
  On the real interval a basis density has modulus below $214$, $\| lambda \| < 3$, and $pi \| z \| < 10$; thus the two exact moments have modulus below $10^4$. Substitution of $H = 10^4$, $q = 7 \/ 4$, $d = 2.12$, and $eta_Q = eta_D = 10^(- 25)$ in (7.6) gives
  $ frac(\( 3.12 + 10^(- 25) \) 1.19 dot.op 10^(- 13) + 10^4 10^(- 25), 7 \/ 4) + frac(3.12 dot.op 10^4 10^(- 25), \( 7 \/ 4 \)^2) < 2.13 dot.op 10^(- 13) . $
  The pointwise majorant $10^53$ for the full domain was proved in Lemma 16. In the second case the total moment radius is at most
  $ 10^53 2^(- 255) + 8 dot.op 10^(- 15) < 1.728 dot.op 10^(- 24) + 8 dot.op 10^(- 15) < 10^(- 14) . $
  For the value and first-derivative moments in (6.11), the real density is bounded by $1000$, the sum of seven atom moduli by $42$, and the two factors by $3$ and $30$. Hence both exact moments are bounded by $H = 4 dot.op 10^4$. The right side of (7.6) is then at most
  $ frac(\( 3.12 + 10^(- 25) \) 10^(- 14) + 4 dot.op 10^4 10^(- 25), 7 \/ 4) + frac(3.12 dot.op 4 dot.op 10^4 10^(- 25), \( 7 \/ 4 \)^2) < 1.8 dot.op 10^(- 14) . $
  All inequalities here are comparisons of the displayed rational bounds. The refinement argument above proves that their coefficient and atom budgets can be met for any prescribed finite set of these moments. ◻
]

For a matrix with $r$ rows and entry error at most $epsilon$, the induced column-norm error is at most $r epsilon$, and multiplication by $W_eta$ adds the factor $parallel W_eta parallel_1$. In particular $r = 6$ for the $J$ row block and $r = 12$ for the rows on $E ∩ \[ 7 \, 16 \]$. In a tabulated residual, the other summands in (6.15) are exact rationals, so its error is just the corresponding pair error. Finally, for the signed half-gaps of Lemma 10, the two moment errors below $10^(- 14)$ give a power-coefficient error below $8 dot.op 10^(- 14)$ and a Bernstein-coefficient error below $2.32 dot.op 10^(- 12)$, as proved in Section 7.3.1. These bounds specify the margins required in a Taylor-based comparison; they do not themselves assert the matrix, residual, or Bernstein values printed in Appendix 6.

=== 7.7 Direct node-Taylor representation <ind:node-taylor>

There is also a local Taylor representation for the direct cardinal function that does not integrate its spectral density. It applies to the full $ell^1$ class of (2.11). Write $cal(N)_0 = { 0 } union cal(N)$ and, for $m in cal(N)_0$, put

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.27)")
 $ rho_m = min_(n in cal(N)_0\
n eq.not m) \| m - n \| . $ <ind:node-radius>]

The consecutive gaps in one period of $cal(N)_0$ are $1 \, 2 \, 1 \, 3 \, 2 \, 3$. Thus the minimum exists and is positive: $rho_m = 1$ for $m = 0$ and for residues $0 \, 1 \, 3 \, 4$, while $rho_m = 2$ for residues $7 \, 9$. This is a guaranteed radius to the nearest other node; it may be smaller than the analytic radius for a particular list whose corresponding coefficients vanish.

#thm([Proposition 7.6.])[
  #emph[Let $p = P R$ have a real $ell^1$ coefficient list as in (2.10). Define 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.28)")
 $ A_k \( m \) = frac(P^(\( k \)) \( m \), k !) = frac(1, k !) sum_(v = - 6)^6 P_v \( i pi t_v \)^k e^(i pi t_v m) . $ <ind:node-P-coefficients>]

 Then $A_0 \( m \) = A_1 \( m \) = 0$. The regular series has coefficients 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.29)")
 $ r_j \( m \) = C upright(bold(1))_({ j = 0 }) + \( - 1 \)^j sum_(n in cal(N)_0\
n eq.not m) (frac(\( j + 1 \) c_n, \( m - n \)^(j + 2)) + frac(d_n, \( m - n \)^(j + 1))) \, $ <ind:node-regular-coefficients>]

 and converges for $\| u \| < rho_m$. For $0 < \| u \| < rho_m$, the Laurent expansion is 
$ R \( m + u \) = c_m / u^2 + d_m / u + sum_(j = 0)^oo r_j \( m \) u^j . $

 For every integer $ell gt.eq 0$ the direct derivative is therefore 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.30)")
 $ frac(p^(\( ell \)) \( m \), ell !) = c_m A_(ell + 2) \( m \) + d_m A_(ell + 1) \( m \) + sum_(k = 2)^ell A_k \( m \) r_(ell - k) \( m \) \, $ <ind:node-derivatives>]

 where the last sum is empty for $ell = 0 \, 1$.]
] <ind:node-taylor-proposition>

#proof[
  For $n eq.not m$, the geometric series for $\( m - n + u \)^(- 1)$ and its derivative give
  $ frac(1, m - n + u) = sum_(j gt.eq 0) frac(\( - 1 \)^j u^j, \( m - n \)^(j + 1)) \, #h(2em) frac(1, \( m - n + u \)^2) = sum_(j gt.eq 0) frac(\( - 1 \)^j \( j + 1 \) u^j, \( m - n \)^(j + 2)) \, $
  whenever $\| u \| < \| m - n \|$. To justify summing these series over the infinite list, fix $0 lt.eq r < rho_m$ and set $q = r \/ rho_m < 1$. Since $\| m - n \| gt.eq rho_m$ for every other node, the sum of absolute values on $\| u \| lt.eq r$ is at most
  $ frac(sum_(n eq.not m) \| c_n \|, rho_m^2) sum_(j gt.eq 0) \( j + 1 \) q^j + frac(sum_(n eq.not m) \| d_n \|, rho_m) sum_(j gt.eq 0) q^j = frac(sum_(n eq.not m) \| c_n \|, rho_m^2 \( 1 - q \)^2) + frac(sum_(n eq.not m) \| d_n \|, rho_m \( 1 - q \)) < oo . $
  This proves absolute uniform convergence on every smaller closed disk, permits exchanging the node and Taylor sums, and gives (7.29). It also proves precisely why the nearest-other-node distance is a valid common radius.
  The finite Fourier expansion (2.5) gives (7.28), and the double zero of $P$ gives $A_0 = A_1 = 0$. Multiply its entire Taylor series by the convergent Laurent series for $R$. The two principal terms are canceled by the double zero; collecting the coefficient of $u^ell$ gives (7.30). In particular $A_2 = Q_m$ and $A_3 = Q_m D_m$, so the cases $ell = 0 \, 1$ reproduce (2.17). ◻
]

For finite rational input, the constants in this direct formula can be enclosed by the rational operations above. For an arbitrary $ell^1$ input the formula is an exact convergence statement; a computable error for truncating its node sum additionally requires a bound for that list’s tail. It represents the direct function $p$ only. The transformed function $K_p$ still has the kernel representation (2.19), and this local identity neither places $K_p$ in the cardinal class nor replaces the exact-derivative certificates for the tabulated functions.


